t and status labels. •	All screen layouts must preserve meaningful reading order and hierarchy at desktop, tablet and mobile widths. 4.2 Visual system specification Token	Direction Primary brand	Deep navy / dark blue; use for navigation, strong headings and primary brand surfaces. Primary action	Restrained blue; one visually dominant primary action per screen where possible. Background	White and very light neutral grey; use subtle section contrast rather than heavy decoration. Positive	Green for successful, healthy, completed and resolved states. Warning	Amber for pending, attention-needed and approaching-limit states. Critical	Red for failed, blocked, unresolved critical risks and destructive actions. Typography	Modern readable sans serif. Page title > section title > card title > body > helper text. Body text	Target comfortable 14–16 px for common UI text; do not make labels/table values tiny. Spacing	Consistent 4/8-based spacing rhythm; consistent card padding and vertical gaps. Charts	Use clear titles, units, legends, accessible labels and filters; never imply unknown metrics. Tables	Readable row height, aligned columns, visible status, useful empty state, pagination only where needed. 4.3 Component inventory •	Navigation: sidebar group, nav item, active state, locked nav item, breadcrumb, topbar, profile menu. •	Data display: KPI card, usage meter, status badge, table, empty state, timeline event, activity feed, chart, scorecard row, stakeholder card. •	Inputs: text, email, password, phone, URL, dropdown, multi-select where required, date range, file upload, search, filter chips, slider, checkbox, radio. •	Actions: primary/secondary/tertiary buttons, icon action, overflow menu, copy, download, retry, rescore, archive, delete, upgrade. •	Overlays: confirmation modal, form modal/drawer, upgrade prompt, permission notice, integration connection dialog, source drawer. •	AI: prompt composer, conversation message, source citation, evidence drawer, suggested prompt, processing indicator, answer feedback only if specified. •	Meeting: recording player, transcript with speaker labels/timestamps, cue evidence panel, objection card, analysis tab set. •	Deal: deal header, stage indicator, deal summary, risk indicator, next-step checklist, stakeholder card, meeting timeline. 5. CUSTOMER JOURNEYS — END TO END 5.1 Start Free journey 7.	Visitor opens SellMore website and selects Start Free. 8.	Registration form collects the source-defined required fields and Terms & Conditions acceptance. 9.	System creates company workspace and Client Admin account (role assignment is a working interpretation to confirm). 10.	Free Plan is assigned with configured limits and entitlements. 11.	Client Admin lands on company dashboard. 12.	Admin may invite team members within the configured seat limit, upload company documents, view predefined QA prompts and manually upload meetings. 13.	Paid-only/locked features remain visible as locked where the PRD requires it and lead to plan comparison. 14.	Free Plan meeting allowance is maximum 10 records; show used/remaining usage. 5.2 Paid purchase journey 15.	New visitor selects a paid plan or existing user opens Pricing/Billing. 16.	Show plan comparison: subscription name, price, billing cycle, included credits, Analyse limits, team seats, modules and integrations. 17.	Route to Register or Sign In; existing users must be mapped to their existing company workspace. 18.	Show order summary with selected plan, billing cycle, price adjustments, taxes/discounts where applicable and total payable. 19.	Customer proceeds to configured payment gateway. 20.	Show payment pending/success/failure states. 21.	Activate paid plan only after successful payment confirmation. 22.	Update entitlements, credits, usage limits, seats, modules and integrations. 23.	Retain historical meetings and analysis on upgrade. 24.	Existing Free Plan remains until paid activation succeeds; existing paid subscription remains governed by current subscription until approved plan-change rules take effect. 5.3 Closed-loop sales execution Stage	User/system activity	UI output Acquire context	Connect CRM or use permitted manual context	Lead/contact/company/deal context Prepare	Deal Assistance, Deal Coaching, Knowledge Base where entitled	Preparation questions, context and guidance Conduct	Real sales conversation; Live Assist where entitled	Transcript, notes and contextual suggestions Capture	Save/import/upload meeting	Canonical meeting record Analyse	Generate summary, cue scores, objections and improvements	Evidence-backed report Improve	Practice and Lessons	Skill practice Simulate	Mock Meeting / Roleplay	Roleplay transcript, score and feedback Follow up	Review and confirm suggested actions	Tasks/communications; CRM sync if configured Repeat	Next real meeting and new analysis	Longitudinal performance evidence OPTIONAL BRANCHES  Practice and Mock Meetings are optional, not mandatory steps. A representative may proceed directly to the next real meeting. 6. ACCOUNT, AUTHENTICATION AND SUBSCRIPTION SCREENS 6.1 Registration screen Purpose: Create a company workspace and Client Admin account. Primary users: New visitor Entry point: Website → Start Free or paid plan checkout → Register Required layout and content •	Centered form within branded public/auth shell; concise title and explanation; clear required/optional labels; terms acceptance; link to Sign In. Fields, controls and actions •	Full Name (required); Business Email (required); Password (required); Confirm Password (required); Company Name (required); Industry / Domain Type dropdown (required); Company Website (optional); Contact Number (required); Terms & Conditions checkbox (required). Interaction and behaviour 25.	Validate email format; uniqueness/verification rules remain TBD. Confirm Password must match Password. Validate URL if supplied. Country code and phone rules TBD. Do not invent password policy or exact validation copy. States to design •	Default; field focu